This assert statement is related to the comment that starts with "@@@ Size of list decreases by one or remains the same after remove". It asserts that the size of the list before and after the call to `Remove(Object obj)` is the same or one less.
--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(this.Count) - 1 >= 0);
</code>

This assert statement checks whether the Count property of the list decreases by at least one or remains the same after the Remove method is called. The reason we check for ">= 0" is because the Count shouldn't go below zero.
--------------------
Debug.Assert(RET >= 0); // @@@ array list size should always be non-negative
</code>
--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(ArrayList.Count) - 1 == ArrayList.Count);
</code>
In the provided code, there is a Remove method without return value. Thus, it is difficult to create an assert statement for the "ArrayList size decreases by one or remains the same after remove" comment as it would not have any meaningful value to compare against. The Count property of ArrayList is a read-only property, so it cannot be modified directly. The decrease of Count is accomplished by removing an item, which is the main reason for its declaration. 

Therefore, it would be more practical to assert that the Count property increases after an ArrayList item is added (by the Add method), assuming that the "Add" method indeed increases the Count by one. Assuming the code is as follows:

```cs
public virtual int Add(Object value)
{
    if (_size == _items.Length) EnsureCapacity(_size + 1);
    _items[_size] = value;
    _version++;
    return _size++;
}
```
Then the assert statement could be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(OLD(ArrayList.Count) + 1 == ArrayList.Count);
```
